\f1\fs26 Sarah locked her office door and sat so fast at her desk, you\'92d think it was a sports car. She raced to warn Mr Fairy Boxers before the dark man with a gun and ominous present-tense sentences, found him. She knew she should care nothing for a Cloudborne, especially a manual labourer, but there was no shaking the feeling she may have, in some indirect way, set the dark man on his trail. 
\f2\i What is this feeling? Is it guiltiness? No, 
\f1\i0 she decided. 
\f2\i It\'92s worse. It\'92s embarrassment.
\f1\i0 \
She stroked her holoscreen and smiled. Let others deride it, dismiss it, and never suspect its capabilities. She made it throw a dizzying forest of 3-dimensionally rendered business documents around her, then blinked a small console alive in the shade of them. From a drawer she produced a keyboard\'97yes, a keyboard!\'97and discreetly typed. Faster than the executive overviews around her could render an insomniac unconscious, she was inside office security, downloading surveillance logs and reconstituting half-overwritten image streams. Glee, however, dissolved to pursed-lipped disappointment when she found no trace of the dark man among the compromised data. There was no evidence of him in reception, no record of his awkward marching in the hallway, no account of his reptilian slithering in Suicide Dude\'92s insufficiently sterile office. No gun. No menace. Sarah ran the logs through image processors looking for editing artefacts. Nothing. The spaces where the dark man had been became infused, instead, with Sarah\'92s fear.\
Scrambling security footage like this required rare sophistication. That the dark man had such support, marked him as a probable PAPER agent. 
\f2\i Tax me into poverty! I sent this loon after Mr Fairy Boxers.
\f1\i0 \
